rns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**ETF liquidation ≠ losing everything.** When a US '40 Act ETF closes, the fund sells its holdings, pays expenses, and distributes remaining net assets pro rata to shareholders, usually in cash. Your loss is limited to any NAV decline. A total loss would require the underlying assets to become worthless, which is rare for diversified funds.\n\n**Amount:** You receive your share of NAV at liquidation, after costs, which may be above or below your purchase price.\n\n**Timing:** The sponsor announces a last trading day and a later distribution date. Schedules vary, so check the fund's notice or prospectus supplement. You can also sell on-exchange before delisting, possibly at wider spreads.\n\n**Taxes (taxable accounts):** Liquidation generally counts as a sale, realizing a short- or long-term capital gain or loss versus your cost basis. A final distribution may include separately taxed capital gains or income.","ttft_ms":1161,"type":"result","duration_ms":3253,"uuid":"ad2fd72c-f6a9-492a-89ce-07f29eb48280","ttft_stream_ms":1063,"time_to_request_ms":68,"first_content_frame_ms":1063,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
